An Irq holds its notification with the capability's bits in the second word, as a table holds one,
so a revoke above it leaves the Irq armed;
an Irq's notification has no bits of its own.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: irq's notification slot names no live notification

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -740,7 +740,7 @@ static int op_irq_line(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
          * It hangs below the capability it was bound with, so a revoke above that disarms the Irq,
          * with b 0, as no Notification capability has; the bits it may signal lie in the Irq.
          */
-        irq->ntfn = (struct cap){ .type = CAP_NOTIFICATION, .rights = nc.rights, .a = nc.a };
+        irq->ntfn = (struct cap){ .type = CAP_NOTIFICATION, .rights = nc.rights, .a = nc.a, .b = nc.b };
         irq->may = nc.b;
         cap_attach(slot_node(t, arg[2]), &irq->ntfn);
         irq->line = first;
